WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

REGIONAL REGULATION OF OGAN KOMERING ULU REGENCY

NUMBER 1 OF 2022

CONCERNING

WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

THE REGENT OF OGAN KOMERING ULU,

Considering:

a. that every woman and child has the right to survival, growth, and proper development and has the right to protection from violence and discrimination as mandated in the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
b. that discriminatory treatment and violence against women and children will have an impact on the quality of human resources in the future;
c. that in order to realize the vision and mission of development in the field of women's empowerment and child protection, respect, protection, fulfillment, enforcement, and advancement of the rights of women and children, it is deemed necessary to establish policies that can ensure the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 28 of 1959 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions and City Governments of South Sumatra (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1959 Number 73, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1821);
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning the Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
7. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4967);
8. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 144, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);
9. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
10. Government Regulation Number 61 of 2014 concerning Reproductive Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 169, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5559);
11. Government Regulation Number 78 of 2021 concerning Special Protection for Children (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2021 Number 174, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6704);
12. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 15 of 2008 concerning General Guidelines for the Implementation of Gender Mainstreaming in the Regions as has been amended by Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 67 of 2011 concerning Amendment to Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 15 of 2008 concerning General Guidelines for the Implementation of Gender Mainstreaming in the Regions (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 927);
13. Regulation of the Minister of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 7 of 2013 concerning Guidelines for Increasing Women's Political Participation in Legislative Institutions (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2013 Number 1482);

With Mutual Approval

THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF OGAN KOMERING ULU REGENCY

and

THE REGENT OF OGAN KOMERING ULU

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION.

Article 10

Women's empowerment in the field of health is implemented by means including:
a. facilitating women's health through health program activities implemented by the government such as:
1\. Maternal mortality reduction program;
2\. Maternal and child health (KIA) program;
3\. Healthy environment, water sanitation and sanitation program;
4\. Malaria eradication program;
5\. Pulmonary tuberculosis eradication program;
6\. HIV/AIDS prevention program;
7\. Community nutrition improvement program;
b. Women are given the right to obtain special protection in the implementation of their work or profession against matters that may threaten their safety and/or health;
c. Women are given special rights inherent to women due to their reproductive function, guaranteed and protected by law.

Article 11

Women's empowerment in the field of employment is implemented by means including:
a. equal wages for male and female workers for the same work;
b. prohibition of discrimination against female workers;
c. prohibition of termination of employment for female workers who marry, become pregnant, and give birth;
d. guidelines for employing female workers at night;
e. providing flexibility for female workers who breastfeed children;
f. prohibition of discrimination against female workers in company regulations; and
g. regarding malnutrition in female workers.

Article 12

Women's empowerment in the field of politics is implemented by means including:
a. facilitating the improvement of human resources for prospective legislative candidates at the regency level, and members of political parties/women's community organizations in order to fulfill women's representation in legislative institutions in accordance with laws and regulations;
b. facilitating the increase of women's political participation in legislative institutions;
c. providing facilities and infrastructure for women's community organizations or community organizations whose vision and mission is to fight for the fulfillment of rights and protection of women in the Regency in accordance with Regional Financial capacity;
d. implementing socialization in order to increase awareness of women's rights and women's participation in the field of politics;
e. encouraging increased involvement of women in community organizations and decision-making activities within the Regency Government; and
f. encouraging the establishment of women's organizations/forums.

Article 20

Provision of services and advanced referral handling as referred to in Article 17 letter b shall be intended for:
a. women victims of domestic violence;
b. women victims of criminal acts of trafficking in persons;
c. women in emergency situations and special conditions; and
d. women victims of other acts of violence requiring coordination at the Regency level.

Article 23

Strengthening of institutions providing women's protection services shall be carried out by:
a. establishment of a coordination forum or other forums in order to support the handling of victims of violence against women;
b. provision of service units and legal protection assistance for women victims of violence who conduct data collection related to violence against women;
c. provision of information education communication, advocacy, socialization, and technical guidance;
d. procurement of facilities and infrastructure for protection and services for women in disaster and evacuation areas;
e. enhancement of cross-sectoral coordination;
f. establishment of working groups;
g. formulation of regional action plans; and
h. information education communication both through electronic and print media and simulation regarding women's protection.

Article 24

1. In the context of implementing Child Protection, the Regency Government shall be obliged to carry out activities:
a. formulation of policies, programs, and regional development activities with child rights perspective which are set forth in regional development planning documents;
b. formulation of norms, standard procedures and assessment criteria for the fulfillment and protection of children's rights which refer to the norms, standards, procedures and criteria established by the Government;
c. coordination of child-friendly Regency development shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
d. facilitation of the implementation of regional children's forums;
e. enhancement of Community participation in the implementation and protection of children's rights;
f. coordination and cooperation in the fulfillment and protection of Children;
g. monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of programs for the fulfillment and protection of Children;
h. guidance of child protection institutions; and i. provision of special protection for Children.
2. In addition to the activities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regency Government may conduct supervision and facilitation of child advocacy institutions in the Regency involved in assistance to Children requiring special handling.
3. Supervision and facilitation as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
4. Provision of facilitation as referred to in paragraph (3) shall be adjusted to the regional financial capacity.

Article 29

1. The Regency Government is obliged to fulfill the rights of children in the fields of:
a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care;
c. basic health and welfare; and
d. education, use of leisure time, and arts and cultural activities.
2. In fulfilling the rights of children as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a, the Regency Government is obliged to:
a. endeavor for children to obtain birth certificates free of charge;
b. provide infrastructure and facilities needed by children to express themselves, think, and create; and
c. provide infrastructure and facilities so that children can obtain the knowledge they need.
3. In fulfilling the rights of children as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, the Regency Government undertakes efforts to:
a. provide guidance to families or substitute families to fulfill the rights of children needed during their care;
b. provide family consultation institutions for child caregivers;
c. provide child social welfare institutions as an alternative place for child caregivers in the event that families or substitute families cannot provide proper care; and
d. provide guidance to children as vulnerable groups.
4. In fulfilling the rights of children as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, the Regency Government undertakes efforts to:
a. reduce infant mortality rates;
b. reduce malnutrition rates for children;
c. provide breast milk for infants;
d. provide immunization for children;
e. provide reproductive health services;
f. increase access to welfare improvement for poor families;
g. increase access to clean water for households;
h. create smoke-free zones; and
i. child-friendly community health centers.
5. In fulfilling the rights of children as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, the Regency Government undertakes efforts to:
a. provide early childhood education;
b. implement 12 years of compulsory education;
c. establish child-friendly schools;
d. establish safe routes to/from school; and
e. provide child-friendly creative and recreational activity facilities;
6. Further provisions regarding the form and procedures for fulfilling the rights of children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 31

1. The Regency Government is obliged and responsible to carry out prevention and handling of cases of child victims of neglect, violence, discrimination, exploitation, child trafficking, maltreatment, narcotics, psychotropics, and other addictive substances, and/or pornography.
2. The responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. respecting, fulfilling, and protecting the rights of children;
b. conducting socialization about the adverse effects of neglect, violence, discrimination, exploitation, child trafficking, maltreatment, narcotics, psychotropics, and other addictive substances, and/or pornography;
c. making policies and/or policy changes regarding child protection;
d. providing assistance;
e. conducting training on handling cases of neglect, violence, discrimination, exploitation, child trafficking, maltreatment, narcotics, psychotropics, and other addictive substances, and/or pornography against children for stakeholders;
f. assisting the social rehabilitation and social reintegration process;
g. providing temporary shelters for children; and
h. conducting coordination and cooperation with stakeholders related to handling child cases.

Article 32

1. The Regency Government is obliged and responsible to provide special protection to child victims of violence which includes:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are economically and/or sexually exploited;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and addictive substance abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. child victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. child victims of physical and/or mental violence;
j. child victims of sexual crimes;
k. child victims of terrorism networks;
l. children with disabilities;
m. child victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
2. Special Protection for Children is carried out through efforts to:
a. provide rapid handling, including treatment and/or physical, psychological, and social rehabilitation, as well as prevention of diseases and other health disorders;
b. provide psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery;
c. provide social assistance for Children from families who are unable; and
d. provide protection and assistance in every judicial process.
3. Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be provided at technical implementing units of ministries/agencies, PD, and/or regional technical implementing units that have been established with reference to the service standards that have been set.
4. Special Protection for Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented quickly, comprehensively, and in an integrated manner.

Article 33

The Regency Government in implementing Special Protection as referred to in Article 32 shall provide:
a. social workers and social welfare personnel;;
b. competent and trained health personnel;
c. religious/worship guidance officers;
d. educators and education personnel; and/or
e. legal aid personnel.

Article 35

1. Special Protection as referred to in Article 34 paragraph (1) shall be carried out through:
a. prevention so that Children do not become victims in emergency situations;
b. recording the number of Children requiring Special Protection in emergency situations;
c. mapping the basic and specific needs of Children requiring Special Protection in emergency situations;
d. guarantee of security and safety of Children in Emergency Situations;
e. recording of Children and their families for tracing and family reunification;
f. priority emergency rescue, evacuation, and security measures;
g. physical and psychological health recovery;
h. provision of legal aid, assistance, physical, psychological, and social rehabilitation for Children in Emergency Situations;
i. care;
j. improvement of facilities needed by Children in Emergency Situations;
k. fulfillment of basic and special needs of Children consisting of food, clothing, shelter, education, provision of health services, learning and recreation, security guarantees, and equal treatment;
l. fulfillment of special needs for Children with Disabilities and Children experiencing psychosocial problems;
m. exemption from education fees both conducted in formal and non-formal Educational Institutions during the emergency period; and
n. provision of services for fulfillment of Children's identity rights and important documents lost due to emergency situations; and/or provision of Social Reintegration services.
2. Special Protection for Children in Emergency Situations as referred to in paragraph (1) must be received by Children in Emergency Situations as soon as possible, the implementation of which shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
3. Provision of services for fulfillment of Children's identity rights and important documents lost due to emergency situations as referred to in paragraph (1) letter n shall be carried out by the Regency Government.

Article 36

1. Special Protection for Children in Conflict with the Law as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (1) letter b shall be carried out through:
a. humane treatment with attention to needs according to their age;
b. separation from adults;
c. provision of legal aid and other assistance effectively;
d. implementation of recreational activities;
e. freedom from torture, punishment, or other treatment that is cruel, inhumane, and degrading to dignity and degree;
f. avoidance of imposition of death penalty and/or life imprisonment;
g. avoidance of arrest, detention or imprisonment, except as a last resort and for the shortest period of time;
h. provision of trial before a Children's court that is objective, impartial, and in a closed session to the public.  i. avoidance of publication of their identity;
j. provision of accompaniment by parents/guardians and persons trusted by the Child;
k. provision of social advocacy;
l. provision of private life;
m. provision of accessibility, especially for Children with Disabilities;
n. provision of education;
o. provision of health services; and
p. provision of other rights in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
2. Special Protection for Children in Conflict with the Law as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through efforts of:
a. prevention;
b. administrative case resolution;
c. rehabilitation; and
d. social reintegration.

Article 38

1. Prevention efforts so that children do not come into conflict with the law as referred to in Article 36 paragraph (2) letter a shall be carried out so that:
a. Children do not become victims of criminal acts
b. Children do not come into conflict with the Law; and
c. Children do not commit criminal acts again.
2. Prevention so that children do not come into conflict with the Law as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through:
a. print media, electronic media, and online media;
b. face-to-face in the form of counseling, discussions, lectures, campaigns; and
c. outdoor media.
3. Prevention efforts so that Children do not come into conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph 1 letter b shall be carried out through:
a. dissemination;
b. providing places or spaces for play, healthy recreation, and channeling Children's creativity;
c. providing guidance on religious values and social values;
d. conducting supervision of the environment that will have an impact on the occurrence of Children in conflict with the Law;
e. increasing family resilience and welfare;
f. providing guidance and skills training;
g. providing places or spaces for play, healthy recreation, and channeling Children's creativity;
h. providing religious guidance, social values, and good character;
i. developing an environment that cares for Children in Conflict with the Law; and/or
j. involving families in service, guidance, and mentoring programs.

Article 47

Involvement of various companies, labor unions, non-governmental organizations, and the Community in the elimination of exploitation of Children economically and/or sexually as referred to in Article 44 letter c shall be carried out through:
a. formulation of policies on the elimination of economic and/or sexual exploitation of Children in their environment;
b. cooperation to prevent Children from being exploited economically and/or sexually;
c. campaigns for the elimination of economic and/or sexual exploitation of Children;
d. increased supervision to prevent Children from being exploited economically and/or sexually;
e. company support for the elimination of exploitation of Children through corporate social responsibility; and
f. reporting to the competent authorities if there is exploitation of Children economically and/or sexually.

Article 48

In the event that Children Who Are Exploited Economically and/or Sexually require restoration of their condition to its original state, services must be provided in the form of:
a. medical rehabilitation;
b. Social rehabilitation;
c. legal assistance and social assistance; and/or
d. repatriation and Social Reintegration.

Article 51

Prevention efforts as referred to in Article 49 shall be carried out through:
a. communication, information, and education about the dangers for Children if involved in production and distribution as well as the dangers of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and other addictive substances;
b. increasing the role of parents, families, the Community, educators, education personnel, religious leaders, traditional leaders, Community leaders in supporting the Social Reintegration process of Children Who Are Victims of Narcotics, Alcohol, Psychotropic, and Other Addictive Substance Abuse;
c. providing understanding and awareness to Children regarding the dangers of smoking;
d. providing understanding and awareness to Children about the dangers of Children being involved in production and distribution as well as the dangers of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and other addictive substances;
e. involving Children as peers in order to provide understanding and change mindsets about the dangers of Children being involved in production and distribution as well as the dangers of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and other addictive substances; and
f. inclusion of anti-narcotics, alcohol, psychotropics, and other addictive substances teaching materials in educational units.

Article 56

Guidance as referred to in Article 55 paragraph (1) shall be carried out by the Regency Government in accordance with its authority by:
a. coordinating the prevention and handling of Child pornography;
b. conducting socialization;
c. organizing education and training;
d. increasing public awareness and responsibility; and
e. conducting guidance through institutional and non-institutional systems.

Article 58

Social recovery as referred to in Article 55 paragraph (1) shall be provided in the form of:
a. resocialization;
b. counseling on moral values sourced from religious teachings in accordance with the religion professed by the Child;
c. increasing public awareness to be able to accept Children who are victims or perpetrators of pornography; and
d. periodic monitoring.

Article 59

Physical and mental health recovery as referred to in Article 55 paragraph (1) shall be provided in the form of:
a. psychosocial therapy;
b. counseling;
c. beneficial activities;
d. referral to hospitals, safe houses, service centers, or other alternative places according to needs; and/or
e. resocialization.

Article 60

1. Where necessary, the Regency Government and the Community may undertake prevention efforts so that Children do not become victims of pornography.
2. Prevention efforts by the Regency Government as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out through:
a. formulation and implementation of policies related to regional action plans on the prevention and handling of pornography;
b. optimization of the role of the Task Force for the Prevention and Handling of Pornography;
c. capacity building of the Regional Task Force for the Prevention and Handling of Pornography;
d. formulation and dissemination of communication, information, and education materials related to pornography prevention in schools and the Community;
e. guidance to the Community not to engage in activities leading to pornography; and
f. guidance to Regional Apparatus, non-governmental organizations, and the Community to enhance prevention and handling efforts for pornography.
3. Prevention efforts by the Community so that Children do not become Victims of Pornography through:
a. dissemination of communication, information, and education materials related to pornography prevention in the surrounding environment;
b. strengthening the surrounding environment not to engage in activities leading to pornography; and
c. enhancing prevention and handling efforts for pornography in the surrounding environment.
4. Provisions regarding the procedures for prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (2) and paragraph (3) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 63

Supervision efforts as referred to in Article 62 shall be carried out in the form of:
a. periodic or ad hoc examinations as well as integrated examinations of Children with HIV and AIDS;
b. testing of periodic and/or ad hoc reports related to the prevention, handling, and protection of Children from HIV and AIDS;
c. health surveillance in the form of recording, reporting, and data analysis on mothers, pregnant mothers, or Children indicated with HIV; and
d. monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of prevention, handling, and protection of Children from HIV and AIDS.

Article 64

Prevention efforts as referred to in Article 62 shall be carried out in the form of:
a. formulating and implementing policies related to action plans on the prevention and handling of Children with HIV and AIDS;
b. health promotion to increase public knowledge about the benefits of early detection and transmission of HIV and AIDS as well as increasing the knowledge and responsibility of mothers, pregnant mothers, Children, and married couples;
c. preventing the transmission of HIV and AIDS from mother to Child;
d. preventing Children from using narcotics;
e. early detection by providing HIV and AIDS tests to pregnant mothers in areas with widespread and concentrated HIV and AIDS endemics;
f. early detection by providing HIV and AIDS tests to pregnant mothers with sexually transmitted infections and tuberculosis in low HIV epidemic areas;
g. using sterile medical devices for Children to avoid HIV and AIDS;
h. avoiding blood transfusions contaminated with HIV and AIDS to Children;
i. providing information about HIV and AIDS transmission to Children;
j. changing Children's behavior to avoid HIV and AIDS;
k. keeping Children away from tattooing; and
l. establishing communication, consultation, and information centers on HIV and AIDS at the village level.

Article 73

Prevention as referred to in Article 70 is carried out by:
a. involving the Community in providing Special Protection for Children;
b. increasing understanding related to abduction, sale, and/or trafficking of Children;
c. establishing bilateral and multilateral cooperation, both national and international;
d. increasing family resilience to prevent Children from abduction, sale, and/or trafficking; and
e. increasing the responsibility of the Community, business sector, and mass media to protect Children from abduction, sale, and/or trafficking.

Article 74

Care and rehabilitation as referred to in Article 70 is carried out by:
a. physical and psychological rehabilitation;
b. medical services, such as reproductive tract infections and communicable diseases;
c. mental health rehabilitation; and/or d. Social Rehabilitation.

Article 80

1. Dissemination and socialization of the provisions of laws and regulations that protect Children who are victims of violence as referred to in Article 74 is carried out by the Regency Government.
2. Monitoring and reporting as referred to in Article 78 is carried out by the Regency Government.
3. Implementation of Special Protection as referred to in Article 79 is carried out by:
a. the Regency Government through Regional Apparatus that administer governmental affairs in the field of education;
b. educators;
c. education personnel;
d. families; and/or
e. the Community, in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 81

Special Protection for Child Victims of Sexual Crimes as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (1) letter j is carried out through:
a. education about reproductive health, religious values, and moral values;
b. Social Rehabilitation;
c. psychosocial assistance during treatment until recovery; and
d. provision of protection and assistance at every level of examination starting from investigation, prosecution, up to examination in court hearings.

Article 83

1. Social Rehabilitation as referred to in Article 81 letter b is carried out in the form of:
a. motivation and psychosocial diagnosis;
b. care and nurturing;
c. vocational training and entrepreneurship development;
d. mental spiritual guidance;
e. physical guidance;
f. social guidance and psychosocial counseling;
g. accessibility services;
h. social assistance and aid;
i. resocialization guidance;
j. follow-up guidance; and/or k. referral.
2. In addition to the forms as referred to in paragraph (1), Social Rehabilitation is carried out in the form of:
a. physical therapy;
b. mental spiritual therapy;
c. psychosocial therapy;
d. therapy for livelihood;
e. fulfillment of decent living;
f. accessibility support; and/or
g. other forms that support social functioning.

Article 88

Special Protection for Children Victims of Terrorism Networks as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (1) letter k shall be carried out through efforts to:
a. educate on education, ideology, and nationalism values;
b. counsel on the dangers of terrorism;
c. Social Rehabilitation; and
d. Social Assistance.

Article 89

1. Education efforts on education as referred to in Article 91 letter a shall be carried out through:
a. instilling moral and mental values so as to live in harmony and peace;
b. teaching character education and good manners; and
c. developing potential and personality as well as skills.
2. Education on ideology for Children Victims of Terrorism Networks as referred to in Article 88 letter a shall be carried out by providing understanding of:
a. Pancasila as the state ideology;
b. history, meaning, function of Pancasila as the foundation of the state, philosophy, way of life, and unifier of the nation; and
c. application or actualization of Pancasila in daily life.
3. Education on nationalism values as referred to in Article 88 letter a shall be carried out by providing understanding to:
a. foster love for the nation and homeland;
b. be willing to sacrifice for the nation and state;
c. be proud of the Indonesian nation and homeland;
d. prioritize the interests of the nation and state above other interests;
e. eliminate extremism; and
f. create harmonious, harmonious relationships and strengthen fraternal bonds.

Article 92

1. Social Assistance to Children Victims of Terrorism Networks as referred to in Article 88 letter d shall be provided outside as well as within the criminal justice proceedings for Children.
2. Social Assistance outside the criminal justice proceedings for Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:
a. home visits;
b. conducting assessments;
c. identifying needs;
d. intervention plans;
e. implementing interventions;
f. connecting to institutions that handle Children Victims of Terrorism Networks; and
g. providing reinforcement to Children Victims of Terrorism Networks.

Article 93

Special Protection for Children Victims of Terrorism Networks shall also be provided in the form of:
a. fulfillment of the rights of Children Victims of Terrorism Networks to care and psychological health recovery;
b. medical rehabilitation;
c. reeducation and Social Reintegration; and
d. safety guarantees both physical, mental, and social for Children Victims of Terrorism Networks.

Article 96

Special Protection for Children with Disabilities as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (1) letter 1 shall be carried out through efforts to:
a. treat humanely in accordance with the dignity and rights of Children;
b. fulfill special needs;
c. provide equal treatment with other Children to achieve full social integration as much as possible and individual development; and
d. Social Assistance.

Article 97

Humane treatment in accordance with the dignity and rights of Children as referred to in Article 96 letter a shall be carried out through:
a. fulfillment of the rights of Children with Disabilities;
b. protection from violence;
c. respect for their mental and physical integrity based on equality with others; and
d. care and upbringing by the family or Substitute Family to grow and develop optimally.

Article 104

Prevention as referred to in Article 102 shall be carried out by:
a. providing guidance to parents regarding the rights of Children not to be maltreated and neglected;
b. providing basic needs services;
c. providing access to education; and
d. providing skills training or independent work.

Article 109

Guidance on religious values and social values as referred to in Article 108 shall be provided in the form of:
a. teaching to carry out religious commands in accordance with -46- the beliefs of Children with Deviant Social Behavior;
b. understanding to behave in accordance with norms of decency and propriety;
c. understanding not to commit violence and destruction; and
d. increasing awareness of the surrounding environment.

Article 115

1. Children who have received Special Protection as referred to in Article 113 shall be returned -47- to their family or the Community.
2. Before being returned to the family or the Community as referred to in paragraph (1), the steps that must be taken are:
a. preparing the psychological condition of Children Who Are Victims of Stigmatization from Labeling related to Their Parents' Condition to be willing to be returned to the family or the Community;
b. ensuring the family, Substitute Family, or the Community to accept and no longer label and discriminate against Children Who Are Victims of Stigmatization from Labeling related to Their Parents' Condition;
c. tracing other Substitute Families if there is rejection from the family or the Community;
d. ensuring Children Who Are Victims of Stigmatization from Labeling related to Their Parents' Condition are in a safe, comfortable condition, and their needs are met; and
e. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the development of Children Who Are Victims of Stigmatization from Labeling related to Their Parents' Condition who have been returned to the family, Substitute Family, or the Community.

Article 122

Improving family quality shall be carried out through:
a. prevention of child marriage;
b. fulfillment of children's rights to exclusive breastfeeding;
c. bringing mothers closer to health services;
d. increasing family resilience.

Article 123

Prevention of child marriage as referred to in Article 122 letter a aims to:
a. increase the productivity of girls;
b. develop the potential of girls;
c. prevent pregnancy at too young an age;
d. reduce maternal and infant mortality rates;
e. reduce poverty rates; and
f. prevent the occurrence of domestic violence.

CHAPTER VII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 131

1. The community may participate in organizing the protection of women and the protection of children.
2. Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be implemented in the context of prevention, handling and empowerment, and fulfillment of children's rights.
3. The role of the community in organizing child protection as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:
a. providing information through socialization and education regarding children's rights and laws and regulations concerning children;
b. providing input in the formulation of policies related to child protection;
c. reporting to the competent authorities if violations of children's rights occur;
d. playing an active role in the rehabilitation and social reintegration process for children;
e. conducting monitoring, supervision and being jointly responsible for the organization of child protection;
f. providing facilities and infrastructure and creating a conducive atmosphere for children's growth and development;
g. establishing a Regency area coordination forum;
h. playing an active role in eliminating negative labeling of Children; and
i. providing space for children to participate and express their opinions.
4. The role of community organizations and educational institutions as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be carried out by taking the necessary steps in accordance with their respective duties, functions, and authorities to assist in organizing child protection and providing information.
5. The role of mass media as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be carried out through the dissemination of information and educational materials that are beneficial from the social, cultural, educational, religious and health aspects of children with due regard to the best interests of the Child.
6. The role of the business sector as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be carried out through:
a. corporate policies with a child perspective;
b. products intended for children must be safe for children; and
c. contributing to the fulfillment of children's rights through corporate social responsibility.

CHAPTER XI

ADMINISTRATIVE SANCTIONS

Article 139

1. The head of an educational unit who violates the provisions as referred to in Article 138 may be subject to administrative sanctions.
2. Sanctions as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of:
a. written warning;
b. transfer;
c. demotion;
d. dismissal from position; ore. revocation of license.
3. The imposition of sanctions as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
